diagnosis_text:
A thorough inspection of both border routers (router1, AS 1; router2, AS 2) and the end hosts shows that the network is operating correctly and that no BGP session reset has occurred. The eBGP session between router1 (193.10.11.1) and router2 (193.10.11.2) has been continuously Established since lab startup with standard default timers (hold 180 s / keepalive 60 s). Keepalives are being exchanged punctually every 60 seconds on both sides, zero BGP NOTIFICATION messages have been sent or received, and the "Connections established: 1; dropped: 0" counters confirm the session has never torn down. The BGP RIB table version has remained at 2 (no route churn), 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24 are mutually installed with correct next-hops, and an end-to-end ping from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) completes with 0% packet loss. No cron jobs, background scripts, short timers, firewall rules, or interface errors were found anywhere in the topology. The on-call person's assertion that "the session between the two border routers is resetting itself over and over" is not supported by any evidence: the session counter, the keepalive log, and the notification counter all agree that the session has never flapped. The root cause of the perceived drops is absent from the lab; the network is healthy, and no corrective action is needed on the routers. The user's intermittent issue should be investigated on the end-host side (pc1 application behaviour, client-side timeouts, or conditions outside this topology).
